# OJ Agent-Generated Toolchain

- Right after verifying problem files exist, immediately check availability of `g++` and `python`; if missing, automatically download portable standalone builds from trusted sources and use them.
- Do not depend on pre-written runtime Python/C++ tools shipped by the bundle.
- Generate the required native tooling for the detected operating system.
- Prefer C++20, falling back to C++17.
- Prefer C++ for executable problem/tooling components.
- Protected executions are limited to 1 GiB RAM and 1 second; an active auto-break watchdog terminates runaway code (loops/recursion) and kills the entire process tree.
- Token economy: write dense, compact code (multiple statements per line where practical) and concise variable/function names to conserve tokens.
- Code comments: comment only on functions that truly need explanation; state only logic, received inputs, and return value; write all comments in English.
- Use native OS APIs for enforcement when available.
